    "Keeping legacy publish/tag mutation behavior indefinitely."
  ],
  "command_contract": {
    "release plan": "Read-only. Local use derives a decision from exact qualified package selections. CI uses an explicit merged-release-commit mode to interpret the immutable parent-to-merged Callisto release-PR delta, verify the consumed changesets and version/changelog closure, and embed the result in a transient intent. It does not rerun changeset planning after merge and it neither publishes, tags, pushes, nor creates a forge release.",
    "release inspect": "Read-only. Displays an intent, artifact manifest, state, or receipt with their binding and readiness checks; it does not recompute an unapproved intent as a side effect.",
    "release execute": "The sole production mutation entry point. It consumes explicit intent and, when needed, artifact-manifest inputs, validates them, obtains a fresh ValidatedReleaseIntent and ApplyPermit, and runs the coordinator from SPEC-RELEASE-EXECUTION-012.",
    "release reconcile": "Read-only. Consumes explicit intent/state inputs and performs exact provider observations to report recoverability; it never publishes, tags, pushes, or creates a forge release."
  },
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "Callisto exposes `release plan`, `release inspect`, `release execute`, and `release reconcile` as explicitly named commands or an equally explicit namespaced interface with the four contracts above. `release plan` requires repeated exact qualified package selections or one named documented configuration-selection mode and embeds the resulting decision in the intent. `release execute` requires an explicit durable intent file path; it never searches the checkout, falls back to a default plan, accepts an inline JSON authority string, accepts a new selection, or recomputes a plan after validation fails. It accepts an explicit artifact-manifest path when the intent declares binary slots, and an optional explicit state path; omitting the latter uses SPEC-RELEASE-EXECUTION-012's outside-checkout default. Parse, trust, binding, capability, artifact, attestation, or state validation failures exit non-zero before credentials or mutating adapters are reached.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "`release plan`, `release inspect`, and `release reconcile` are structurally read-only: their reachable command paths cannot call registry publish, Git tag/push, forge-release creation, or any provider effect adapter. `release execute` is structurally the only production CLI route that can acquire an ApplyPermit and dispatch those adapters. Integration tests invoke every command against recording adapters and assert the exact allowed effect set, including that each error path dispatches none.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "During the documented migration window, `plan-publish`, `publish`, `tag --plan`, and `filter-plan` remain available only as clearly labelled read-only compatibility previews. They may render proposed operations but cannot publish, tag, push, write release state, create a forge release, or acquire ApplyPermit. Their help and diagnostics name `release plan`, `release execute`, or `release reconcile` as the replacement as appropriate. A separately documented breaking release removes the legacy commands rather than silently restoring their mutation behavior.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "The repository has a separate least-privilege version-PR flow and a GitHub release workflow with separate plan, build, and execute jobs. The version-PR flow recomputes the generated version/changelog/consumed-changeset delta from the current base branch rather than rebasing a prior generated commit; normally it creates or updates one stable managed release PR and removes exactly the consumed changesets only in that PR. If GitHub's built-in token rejects updating that long-lived managed branch because the base branch has changed a workflow since it diverged, the flow automatically creates a SHA-suffixed replacement managed branch and PR from the same current base and changesets, closes the superseded PR with a link, and retains the replacement for later ordinary updates. This fallback requires no elevated token, never removes changesets from the base branch, and trusts only the canonical managed branch or an exact SHA-suffixed replacement branch from the repository itself. An optional App or fine-grained token may retain the same PR number but is never required for the default flow. The unprivileged release-plan job checks out the exact merged release commit and its parent, proves the reviewed release delta, runs only read-only merged-release-commit planning, and uploads the transient intent file plus its SHA-256 as same-run workflow artifacts. The build job consumes that exact intent, builds the declared binary slots from the matching source commit, verifies its own intent digest, creates a GitHub artifact attestation for each binary with `attestations: write` and `id-token: write` only in that job, and uploads the artifact manifest and binaries as workflow artifacts. The privileged execute job downloads only artifacts from the same workflow run, independently verifies the intent SHA-256, source commit, artifact-manifest bindings, downloaded binary digests, and each binary's GitHub attestation against the configured repository and immutable signer workflow before invoking `release execute`. No decision or intent is committed or recovered from a cache. A plan/build/execute/attestation mismatch fails before registry, Git, or forge credentials are available.",
      "is_error_case": true
